          "type": "turn",
          "question": "I have both asthma and reflux and my cough is worse at night. Which is more likely to be driving it, and what does guidance describe for each?",
          "answer": "**Please book a review with your clinician soon, because your cough is getting worse.** The symptom check found no emergency pattern, but that is not a clearance. If your breathing gets worse, seek medical advice promptly.\n\nNothing in the sources I had can tell you whether asthma or reflux is driving your cough. Only a clinician who knows your history and examines you can judge that, and both can contribute at the same time. Each condition has features that fit a night-time cough:\n\n- **Asthma:** Cough, wheeze and chest tightness that vary in intensity are often worse at night or early in the morning. Cough alone, especially at night, is a recognised way asthma can present. Diagnosis usually needs breathing tests (spirometry), and a normal result on a good day does not rule asthma out.\n- **Reflux (GERD):** Heartburn and regurgitation are the typical signs, often worse after meals and when lying down. Chronic cough is a less typical sign, and guidance warns against assuming reflux is the cause without considering other causes. Raising the head of the bed is one common step for night-time symptoms.\n\nGuidance also differs on asthma treatment. For mild asthma, global strategy guidance favours an inhaled corticosteroid with formoterol as the preferred reliever. Some national panels say a short-acting bronchodilator reliever alongside a regular controller remains appropriate. I can't say which is right for you, so discuss it with your prescriber rather than changing anything yourself.\n\nI could not find evidence on how strong the link is between reflux and night cough, or on whether acid-suppressing medicine helps cough specifically. A second guideline search also failed, so I can't report what cough-specific reflux guidance says.\n\n**Seek urgent care now** if you have any of these:\n- Breathlessness at rest, or difficulty speaking or breathing normally\n- Chest pain, especially with breathlessness or sweating\n- Blue lips, confusion or dizziness\n- Coughing up blood, or a high fever with a worse cough\n- A cough that improves and then returns worse with fever\n- Difficulty or pain when swallowing, vomiting blood, black stools, or weight loss you can't explain\n\nNot medical advice. This is an educational software project. It does not diagnose or treat, and it must not be used for real medical decisions.",
